Task 1: Expose VM Console Through OpenShift Web Console
Subtask 1.1: Access the OpenShift Web Console
Open your web browser and navigate to the OpenShift web console
Log in using your provided credentials
Navigate to the Virtualization section in the left sidebar
Click on VirtualMachines to view available VMs
Subtask 1.2: Locate Your Virtual Machine
In the VirtualMachines list, locate the VM named lab-vm-console
Click on the VM name to open its details page
Verify the VM status shows as Running
If the VM is not running, click the Actions dropdown and select Start
Subtask 1.3: Access Console Settings
In the VM details page, click on the Console tab
Observe the different console access options available:
VNC Console - Graphical console access
Serial Console - Text-based console access
Note the console connection status indicator
Subtask 1.4: Configure Console Access
Click on the YAML tab to view the VM configuration
Locate the console configuration section:
spec:
  template:
    spec:
      domain:
        devices:
          interfaces:
          - name: default
            masquerade: {}
          - name: console
            bridge: {}
Verify that console access is properly configured in the VM specification
Task 2: Access Console via VNC
Subtask 2.1: Enable VNC Console Access
Return to the Console tab in your VM details
Select VNC Console from the console type dropdown
Wait for the console to initialize and display the VM desktop
Subtask 2.2: Test VNC Console Functionality
Once the VNC console loads, you should see the VM's graphical interface
Test mouse and keyboard interaction within the console
Try opening a terminal application within the VM:
Right-click on the desktop
Select Open Terminal or Terminal Emulator
Subtask 2.3: Configure VNC Console Settings
Click on the Settings icon in the console toolbar
Adjust the following settings:
Scale Mode: Set to Fit to Window
Keyboard Layout: Select your preferred layout
Mouse Mode: Choose Relative or Absolute based on preference
Subtask 2.4: Use CLI to Access VNC Console
Open a terminal on your lab machine
Install the VNC viewer if not already available:
# For RHEL/CentOS/Fedora
sudo dnf install tigervnc

# For Ubuntu/Debian
sudo apt-get install tigervnc-viewer
Get the VNC connection details using the OpenShift CLI:
# Login to OpenShift CLI
oc login --server=https://your-openshift-cluster --token=your-token

# Get VM console information
oc get vm lab-vm-console -o yaml | grep -A 10 console

# Create a port-forward to access VNC
oc port-forward svc/virt-vnc-lab-vm-console 5900:5900
Connect using the VNC viewer:
vncviewer localhost:5900
Task 3: Test Connectivity via Remote Desktop
Subtask 3.1: Prepare VM for Remote Desktop Access
Access your VM through the VNC console (from Task 2)
Install and configure RDP server on the VM:
# Update the system
sudo dnf update -y

# Install XRDP server
sudo dnf install xrdp -y

# Enable and start XRDP service
sudo systemctl enable xrdp
sudo systemctl start xrdp

# Configure firewall to allow RDP
sudo firewall-cmd --permanent --add-port=3389/tcp
sudo firewall-cmd --reload
Create a user account for RDP access:
# Create a new user
sudo useradd -m rdpuser
sudo passwd rdpuser

# Add user to necessary groups
sudo usermod -aG wheel rdpuser
Subtask 3.2: Configure Network Access for RDP
Return to the OpenShift web console
Navigate to Networking > Services
Create a new service to expose RDP port:
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: lab-vm-rdp-service
  namespace: default
spec:
  selector:
    vm.kubevirt.io/name: lab-vm-console
  ports:
  - name: rdp
    port: 3389
    targetPort: 3389
    protocol: TCP
  type: ClusterIP
Apply the service configuration:
oc apply -f rdp-service.yaml
Subtask 3.3: Create Port Forward for RDP Access
Set up port forwarding for RDP access:
# Forward local port 3389 to the VM's RDP service
oc port-forward svc/lab-vm-rdp-service 3389:3389
Keep this terminal session open for the duration of your RDP testing
Subtask 3.4: Test Remote Desktop Connection
Open your RDP client application:
For Linux (using Remmina):

# Install Remmina if not available
sudo dnf install remmina

# Launch Remmina
remmina
For Windows (using built-in RDP client):

Press Windows + R
Type mstsc and press Enter
Configure the RDP connection:

Server: localhost:3389
Username: rdpuser
Password: [password you set earlier]
Establish the connection and verify you can access the VM desktop

Subtask 3.5: Test RDP Functionality
Once connected via RDP, test the following:

Open a web browser within the VM
Create and edit a text file
Test copy/paste functionality between your local machine and the VM
Verify audio redirection (if applicable)
Compare the RDP experience with the VNC console access:

Note differences in performance
Observe feature availability
Test responsiveness of each method
Advanced Configuration and Troubleshooting
Console Security Configuration
Configure console access with authentication:
apiVersion: kubevirt.io/v1
kind: VirtualMachine
metadata:
  name: secure-console-vm
spec:
  template:
    spec:
      domain:
        devices:
          console:
            auth:
              secret:
                secretName: console-auth-secret
Create the authentication secret:
# Create secret for console authentication
oc create secret generic console-auth-secret \
  --from-literal=username=admin \
  --from-literal=password=securepassword
Troubleshooting Common Issues
Issue 1: VNC Console Not Loading

Check VM status:
oc get vm lab-vm-console -o wide
Verify console service is running:
oc get svc | grep vnc
Check VM logs:
oc logs virt-launcher-lab-vm-console-xxxxx
Issue 2: RDP Connection Refused

Verify XRDP service status within the VM:
sudo systemctl status xrdp
Check firewall configuration:
sudo firewall-cmd --list-ports
Verify port forwarding is active:
netstat -tlnp | grep 3389
Issue 3: Poor Console Performance

Adjust VNC console settings:

Reduce color depth
Lower screen resolution
Enable compression
Check network connectivity:

ping -c 4 [vm-ip-address]
Verification and Testing
Console Access Verification Checklist
VNC Console Access:

 Can access VM through web console VNC
 Mouse and keyboard input work correctly
 Can resize and scale console window
 Console remains stable during use
RDP Access:

 RDP service is running on the VM
 Can establish RDP connection from external client
 Full desktop functionality is available
 File transfer capabilities work
Security Verification:

 Console access requires proper authentication
 Network access is properly restricted
 Audit logs capture console access events
Performance Testing
Test console responsiveness:
# Run performance test within VM console
stress-ng --cpu 2 --timeout 30s
Monitor resource usage during console access:
# Check VM resource consumption
oc top pod | grep virt-launcher
Lab Cleanup
Remove Created Resources
Stop port forwarding sessions (Ctrl+C in respective terminals)

Remove the RDP service:

oc delete service lab-vm-rdp-service
Clean up authentication secrets:
oc delete secret console-auth-secret
Stop the test VM if no longer needed:
oc patch vm lab-vm-console --type merge -p '{"spec":{"running":false}}'
